# 22 Other solvers and modules {#ch:other}

Part II followed the centered Navier--Stokes solver. The
library contains several other solvers built from the same pieces (the grid,
events, `bcg.h`, `poisson.h`). This chapter says what each one
solves and when to use it; the header file of each is its documentation.

## Passive scalars: tracer.h and diffusion.h

`tracer.h` advects every scalar in the list `tracers` with the
face velocity $\mathbf{u}_f$, using the BCG scheme of [chapter 11](11_advection), in the
`tracer_advection` event. `diffusion.h` solves the implicit
reaction--diffusion equation
$$
\theta\,\partial_t T = \nabla\cdot(D\nabla T) + \beta T + r
$$
with backward Euler, which gives a Poisson--Helmholtz problem solved by the
multigrid solver of [chapter 16](16_multigrid). Together they give an
advection--diffusion equation for a temperature or a concentration:

~~~literatec
#include "navier-stokes/centered.h"
#include "tracer.h"
#include "diffusion.h"

scalar T[];
scalar * tracers = {T};
const face vector kappa[] = {1e-3, 1e-3};   // diffusivity

event init (t = 0) {
  foreach() {
    T[] = exp (- (sq(x - 0.5) + sq(y - 0.5))/sq(0.1 [1]));
    u.x[] = 1.;
  }
}

event tracer_diffusion (i++)
  diffusion (T, dt, kappa);

event end (t = 0.5)
  fprintf (stderr, "i = 

int main() {
  periodic (right);
  periodic (top);
  init_grid (64);
  run();
}
~~~

Run for this guide, it gives $\int T\,dV = 0.0314159$, equal to the initial
integral $\pi\times0.1^2$ (the scheme conserves it), and a peak of 0.823
against the exact $1/(1 + 4\kappa t/w^2) = 0.833$. The box must be
periodic here: a uniform velocity in the default closed box cannot satisfy
$\nabla\cdot\mathbf{u}= 0$ with $u_n = 0$ on the walls, and the pressure solver fails.

For a tracer that should stay on one side of an interface, attach it to the
volume fraction (`f.tracers = {c}`) so that `vof.h` moves it with
the geometric fluxes ([chapter 12](12_momentum) uses this mechanism for momentum).

## Variants of the Navier--Stokes solver

- `navier-stokes/mac.h` is a staggered (MAC) solver for constant
  density: velocity components live on faces, so the projection is exact and
  there is no cell-centred velocity. Simpler and cheaper than
  `centered.h`, but without variable density, embedded boundaries or VOF
  coupling.

- `navier-stokes/double-projection.h` is an option for
  `centered.h`, after Almgren et al. (2000), that adds a second
  projection. With an approximate projection several pressures can be
  defined, and the header explains which one each variant uses.

- `navier-stokes/conserving.h` moves momentum consistently with
  VOF, for large density ratios ([chapter 12](12_momentum)).

- `axi.h` is for axisymmetric flows. $x$ is the axis and $y \ge 0$ the
  radius. It changes the metric fields $c_m$ and $f_m$ so that every operator
  includes the factor $r$ ([chapter 9](09_grid)). Include it before the solver.

## Other interface methods

- `two-phase.h` uses VOF, the method of this guide.

- `two-phase-clsvof.h` couples VOF with a level set: the signed
  distance `d` is advected and kept consistent with the VOF interface.
  Mass conservation is at least as good as pure VOF, and the smooth distance
  function helps some surface-tension formulations (`integral.h`).

- `two-phase-levelset.h` is a pure level set. Its header recommends
  the coupled method instead, because a level set alone does not conserve
  mass well.

## Compressible flows

`all-mach.h` solves the momentum equation together with an evolution
equation for pressure,
$$
\partial_t p + \mathbf{u}\cdot\nabla p = -\rho c^2\nabla\cdot\mathbf{u},
$$
which reduces to $\nabla\cdot\mathbf{u}= 0$ when the sound speed $c$ is infinite (the
default). `momentum.h` builds a two-phase solver on it, and the
`compressible/` folder contains a compressible two-phase solver with an
energy equation and equations of state in separate files
(`NASG.h`, `Mie-Gruneisen.h`). Use these for bubble collapse, shock--interface
interaction and similar problems where compressibility matters.

## Shallow water and layered models

For flows much wider than deep (rivers, coasts, tsunamis, oceans), the
vertical structure can be modelled instead of resolved.

- `saint-venant.h` solves the shallow-water (Saint-Venant) equations for
  depth $h$ and depth-averaged velocity, with topography $z_b$ and wetting and
  drying, using a well-balanced finite-volume scheme. Its header now
  recommends the multilayer solver for most uses.

- `green-naghdi.h` adds the dispersive terms of the Green--Naghdi
  (Serre) equations, for waves that are not long compared with the depth.

- `layered/hydro.h` is the multilayer solver: the water column is
  divided into layers that move vertically with the flow. With one layer it
  is Saint-Venant; with several it resolves vertical shear.

- `layered/nh.h` adds non-hydrostatic pressure to the multilayer
  solver, which gives dispersive waves with a few layers at a fraction of the
  cost of a full Navier--Stokes free-surface simulation. Further modules in
  `layered/` add diffusion, Coriolis, density stratification and remapping.

## Viscoelastic fluids

`log-conform.h` adds a polymer stress $\nabla\cdot\boldsymbol\tau_p$ to
the momentum equation and evolves the conformation tensor in logarithmic
form, which keeps it positive definite at high Weissenberg numbers. It
supports Oldroyd-B and related models; `fene-p.h` provides the FENE-P
model.

## Generic building blocks

For equations that none of the solvers cover, write your own time loop from
the pieces: `run.h` (events and time loop), `timestep.h` (CFL),
`bcg.h` (advection), `poisson.h` (elliptic solves),
`runge-kutta.h` and `predictor-corrector.h` (explicit time
integration of a list of fields). `$BASILISK/examples` contains
reaction--diffusion systems (Brusselator, Ginzburg--Landau) and the game of
life written this way.

